from common import *
mc('S3.1','sl','Locating an element',r'An atom has ground-state configuration $[\mathrm{Ne}]3s^23p^3$. Which location is correct?', ['Period 3, group 3, s block','Period 3, group 15, p block','Period 2, group 15, p block','Period 3, group 13, d block'],1,'The highest occupied shell is n=3, there are five outer-shell electrons, and the last electrons occupy a p sublevel.')
mc('S3.1','sl','An isoelectronic series','Which species is smallest in the isoelectronic series O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺ and Mg²⁺?', ['O²⁻','F⁻','Na⁺','Mg²⁺'],3,'All have ten electrons. Mg²⁺ has the greatest nuclear charge, so its electron cloud is attracted most strongly and is smallest.')
mc('S3.1','sl','An oxide and water','Which equation represents an acidic oxide reacting with water?', ['Na₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH','CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂','SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄','MgO + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂O'],2,'SO₃ is an acidic non-metal oxide and forms sulfuric acid with water. The metal oxides in the other equations are basic.')
mc('S3.1','sl','An unusual oxygen state','What is the oxidation state of oxygen in hydrogen peroxide, H₂O₂?', ['−2','−1','0','+1'],1,'With hydrogen at +1 and a neutral molecule, 2(+1)+2x=0 gives x=−1.')
mc('S3.1','ahl','Removing d-block electrons',r'Which configuration represents $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$? Fe has atomic number 26.', [r'$[\mathrm{Ar}]3d^5$',r'$[\mathrm{Ar}]3d^34s^2$',r'$[\mathrm{Ar}]3d^64s^2$',r'$[\mathrm{Ar}]3d^6$'],0,'Neutral Fe is [Ar]3d⁶4s². Remove the two 4s electrons first and then one 3d electron to obtain [Ar]3d⁵.')
mc('S3.1','ahl','Absorbing visible light','A solution absorbs orange light strongly and appears blue. Which process is consistent with the standard transition-metal colour model?', ['Emission of blue light as the only source of transmitted colour','Absorption promotes a d electron between split d levels','All d electrons are removed from the ion','The ligand permanently gains an electron in every absorption event'],1,'A photon can promote an electron across the ligand-induced d-level energy gap. The remaining transmitted light gives the complementary observed colour.')
mc('S3.2','sl','Naming a branched alcohol','What is the IUPAC name of CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂OH?', ['2-methylpropan-1-ol','2-methylpropan-2-ol','Butan-1-ol','1-methylpropan-2-ol'],0,'The longest chain containing the hydroxy group has three carbons. Number from the OH end: hydroxy at 1 and methyl at 2.')
mc('S3.2','sl','Distinguishing functional groups','Which compound contains both an ester group and an alkene group?', ['CH₃CH₂COOH','CH₂=CHC(=O)OCH₃','CH₃C(=O)CH₂CH₃','CH₃CH₂OCH₂CH₃'],1,'CH₂=CHC(=O)OCH₃ has a C=C bond and a –C(=O)–O– ester linkage.')
mc('S3.2','sl','A secondary alcohol','Which compound is a secondary alcohol?', ['CH₃CH₂CH₂OH','(CH₃)₃COH','CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃','CH₃OH'],2,'The OH-bearing carbon in butan-2-ol is bonded to two other carbons. Classification is determined at that carbon, not by total chain length.')
mc('S3.2','ahl','Optical isomerism','Which compound contains a carbon bonded to four different groups?', ['Propan-2-ol','Butan-2-ol','2-methylpropan-2-ol','Butan-2-one'],1,'The second carbon in butan-2-ol is bonded to H, OH, CH₃ and CH₂CH₃, so it is a stereogenic centre.')
mc('S3.2','ahl','Proton environments','How many proton environments are present in CH₃COCH₃, ignoring isotope effects?', ['1','2','3','6'],0,'The two methyl groups are related by symmetry, and all six methyl protons are equivalent on the NMR timescale.')
mc('S3.2','ahl','Recognizing an IR combination','A compound shows a strong absorption near 1710 cm⁻¹ and a very broad band spanning approximately 2500–3300 cm⁻¹. Which functional group best fits both observations?', ['Ketone','Carboxylic acid','Ether','Nitrile'],1,'The strong band indicates C=O; the very broad O–H band in this region is characteristic of a carboxylic acid. A ketone lacks the broad O–H band.')
wr('S3.1','sl','A period-3 radius trend','Rounded atomic radii are supplied.'+table(['Element','Na','Mg','Al','Si'],[['Radius / pm',186,160,143,118]]),[('Explain the overall decrease across these elements.',[('R1','Nuclear charge increases while electrons enter the same main energy level.'),('R1','Shielding changes relatively little, so effective attraction increases and the electron cloud contracts.')]),('Explain why Na⁺ is smaller than neutral Na.',[('R1','Formation of Na⁺ removes the outer-shell electron, leaving one fewer occupied shell.'),('R1','There is also less electron–electron repulsion for the same nuclear charge.')])],kind='data-based',difficulty='medium',figure=plot([('',[(11,186),(12,160),(13,143),(14,118)],'#186c93')],'Atomic number','Atomic radius / pm',ylim=(100,200)))
wr('S3.1','sl','Halogen displacement evidence','Aqueous halogen X₂ displaces Y⁻ and Z⁻ from their salts. Y₂ displaces Z⁻ but not X⁻. No other information is needed.',[('Rank X₂, Y₂ and Z₂ from strongest to weakest oxidizing agent and justify your order.',[('A1','X₂ > Y₂ > Z₂.'),('R1','A halogen that displaces another halide accepts electrons from that halide more readily.')]),('Write an ionic equation for X₂ displacing Y⁻ and identify which species is oxidized.',[('A1','X₂ + 2Y⁻ → 2X⁻ + Y₂.'),('A1','Y⁻ is oxidized to Y₂.')])],kind='data-based',difficulty='medium')
wr('S3.1','sl','An amphoteric oxide',r'Aluminium oxide reacts with acids and with strong alkalis.',[('Explain the term amphoteric and write its reaction with dilute hydrochloric acid.',[('R1','It can show both acidic and basic behaviour, reacting with both bases and acids.'),('A1',r'$\mathrm{Al_2O_3+6HCl\rightarrow2AlCl_3+3H_2O}$.')]),('Describe the general change in oxide character across period 3 from sodium to sulfur.',[('R1','The character changes from basic metal oxides through amphoteric aluminium oxide to acidic non-metal oxides.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S3.1','sl','Alkali metal reactivity','Lithium and potassium both react with water. Consider equal-sized clean pieces under otherwise comparable conditions.',[('Write the balanced equation for potassium with water and state an observation consistent with gas formation.',[('A1','2K + 2H₂O → 2KOH + H₂.'),('A1','Effervescence/bubbling; the gas is hydrogen.')]),('Explain why potassium usually reacts more vigorously than lithium.',[('R1','Potassium’s outer electron is in a shell farther from the nucleus and is more shielded.'),('R1','It is therefore removed more readily despite the larger nuclear charge.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S3.1','ahl','Two ionization-energy anomalies','The first ionization energies of Mg and Al are approximately 738 and 578 kJ mol⁻¹; those of P and S are 1012 and 1000 kJ mol⁻¹.',[('Explain why Al has a lower first ionization energy than Mg.',[('R1','The electron removed from Al is in 3p, whereas that from Mg is in 3s.'),('R1','The 3p electron is higher in energy / less strongly held, outweighing the increase in nuclear charge.')]),('Explain why S has a lower first ionization energy than P.',[('R1','Sulfur’s 3p⁴ configuration contains a paired pair in one p orbital; phosphorus 3p³ has singly occupied orbitals.'),('R1','Electron–electron repulsion within the pair makes an electron easier to remove from sulfur.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('S3.1','ahl','Why zinc salts are usually colourless',r'Compare $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ in their common aqueous complexes. Atomic numbers are Zn 30 and Cu 29.',[('Give their d-electron configurations.',[('A1','Zn²⁺: 3d¹⁰.'),('A1','Cu²⁺: 3d⁹.')]),('Use these configurations to explain their usual colour contrast within the d–d transition model.',[('R1','The full d subshell of Zn²⁺ lacks a vacancy for the usual transition between split d orbitals.'),('R1','Cu²⁺ has a partially occupied d subshell, so visible-light absorption between split levels can occur. Do not claim that all colour mechanisms are d–d transitions.')])])
wr('S3.1','ahl','A ligand changes the colour','Two complexes of the same metal in the same oxidation state absorb most strongly at 600 nm and 450 nm. Use h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.00×10⁸ m s⁻¹.',[('Determine which complex has the larger d-level energy gap and calculate the ratio of the two gaps.',[('R1','The complex absorbing 450 nm has the larger gap because E=hc/λ.'),('A1','Gap(450)/gap(600)=600/450=1.33.')]),('Calculate the photon energy at 450 nm and explain why changing a ligand can change the colour.',[('A1','E=(6.63×10⁻³⁴)(3.00×10⁸)/(450×10⁻⁹)=4.42×10⁻¹⁹ J.'),('R1','Different ligands produce different d-level splitting, changing the wavelengths absorbed and therefore the transmitted colour.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('S3.1','ahl','Comparing oxidation states',r'Iron forms $\mathrm{FeCl_2}$ and $\mathrm{FeCl_3}$.',[('Determine the oxidation state and d-electron configuration of iron in each compound.',[('A1','FeCl₂: Fe(+2), [Ar]3d⁶.'),('A1','FeCl₃: Fe(+3), [Ar]3d⁵.')]),('Explain why first-row transition metals commonly exhibit more than one oxidation state.',[('R1','Their 3d and 4s electrons are relatively close in energy.'),('R1','Different numbers of these electrons can participate in bonding or be removed without an immediate very large energy jump.')])])
wr('S3.2','sl','Formula versus connectivity','Two liquids both have molecular formula C₃H₈O.',[('Give a condensed structural formula and IUPAC name for each of the two alcohol isomers.',[('A1','CH₃CH₂CH₂OH: propan-1-ol.'),('A1','CH₃CH(OH)CH₃: propan-2-ol.')]),('Give a third structural isomer with a different functional group, and explain why it is a structural rather than a stereoisomer.',[('A1','CH₃OCH₂CH₃: methoxyethane, an ether.'),('R1','Its atoms are connected differently; stereoisomers retain the same connectivity.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S3.2','sl','Classifying an amine',r'Consider $\mathrm{CH_3CH_2NHCH_3}$.',[('Classify the amine as primary, secondary or tertiary, and explain your choice.',[('A1','Secondary amine.'),('R1','Nitrogen is bonded to two carbon-containing groups and one hydrogen.')]),('Explain why this compound can hydrogen-bond with water and why the word secondary does not specify the number of carbon atoms in its longest chain.',[('R1','Its N–H bond can donate a hydrogen bond and its nitrogen lone pair can accept one from water.'),('R1','Amine classification counts organic groups directly bonded to N, not chain length.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S3.2','sl','Choosing a parent chain','A condensed formula is CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃.',[('State the IUPAC name and molecular formula.',[('A1','3-methylpentane.'),('A1','C₆H₁₄.')]),('Explain why 2-ethylbutane is not the preferred name for this connectivity.',[('R1','A five-carbon continuous chain is present.'),('R1','The parent chain must use the longest appropriate continuous carbon chain, rather than a four-carbon chain with an ethyl branch.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S3.2','ahl','A geometrical isomer','The displayed alkene is shown in the plane of the page.',[('Name the displayed isomer and explain why this alkene can show cis–trans isomerism.',[('A1','cis-but-2-ene.'),('R1','Each double-bond carbon has two different substituents and rotation about C=C is restricted.')]),('Explain why but-1-ene does not show this form of isomerism.',[('R1','One carbon of its double bond is bonded to two identical hydrogen atoms.'),('R1','Exchanging those identical substituents does not produce a distinct configuration.')])],figure=molecule([(280,170,'C'),(440,170,'C'),(180,80,'CH₃'),(540,80,'CH₃'),(180,260,'H'),(540,260,'H')],[(0,1,2),(0,2,1),(1,3,1),(0,4,1),(1,5,1)],'Alkene geometry shown in the plane'))
wr('S3.2','ahl','Optical activity and a racemate','A sample of pure butan-2-ol rotates plane-polarized light. Another sample containing equal amounts of its two enantiomers gives no net rotation.',[('Identify the stereogenic carbon and list its four attached groups.',[('A1','Carbon 2.'),('A1','H, OH, CH₃ and CH₂CH₃.')]),('Explain why the second sample gives no net rotation even though each molecule is chiral.',[('R1','The two enantiomers rotate plane-polarized light by equal magnitudes in opposite directions under identical conditions.'),('R1','Equal amounts cause those effects to cancel; the mixture is racemic.')])])
wr('S3.2','ahl','Identifying an ester by proton NMR',r'A compound has molecular formula $\mathrm{C_3H_6O_2}$ and a strong IR carbonyl absorption. Constructed $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR data are given; exchange effects can be ignored.'+table(['δ / ppm','Integral','Splitting'],[(1.3,3,'triplet'),(4.2,2,'quartet'),(8.1,1,'singlet')]),[('Deduce a structure and assign all three signals.',[('A1','Ethyl methanoate, HCOOCH₂CH₃.'),('A1','1.3 ppm: terminal CH₃; 4.2 ppm: OCH₂; 8.1 ppm: H–C(=O)–.')]),('Explain the splitting of the 1.3 ppm and 4.2 ppm signals.',[('R1','CH₃ has two equivalent neighbouring protons, giving a triplet by n+1.'),('R1','OCH₂ has three equivalent neighbouring protons, giving a quartet.')])],kind='data-based',figure=plot([('',[(1.3,3),(4.2,2),(8.1,1)],'#186c93')],'Chemical shift / ppm','Integral (schematic)',xlim=(10,0),ylim=(0,4),title='Constructed signal positions and integrals',sticks=True))
wr('S3.2','ahl','Combining IR and molecular mass','A compound containing C, H and O has molecular ion m/z=58, a strong IR band near 1715 cm⁻¹, no broad O–H band, and only one proton NMR environment.',[('Deduce the structure, assuming it is a saturated acyclic aldehyde or ketone.',[('M1','The molecular mass is consistent with C₃H₆O (3×12+6×1+16=58).'),('A1','Propanone, CH₃COCH₃.')]),('Explain how each spectroscopic observation supports the assignment.',[('R1','The C=O absorption supports a carbonyl compound, while absence of broad O–H excludes an alcohol/acid assignment.'),('R1','The two methyl groups in propanone are equivalent, so it has one proton environment; propanal would have several.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('S3.2','ahl','A chlorine isotope pair','A constructed mass spectrum of a compound containing exactly one chlorine atom has molecular-ion peaks at m/z=64 and 66 with approximate intensities 3:1. Use C=12, H=1 and chlorine isotope masses 35 and 37.',[('Explain the two peaks and their relative intensities.',[('R1','Molecules containing ³⁵Cl give the lower-mass molecular-ion peak and those containing ³⁷Cl give the higher one.'),('R1','The 3:1 ratio reflects approximately 75% ³⁵Cl and 25% ³⁷Cl.')]),('Deduce a molecular formula if the molecule is a saturated acyclic chloroalkane, and give a plausible formula for a fragment at m/z=29.',[('A1','C₂H₅Cl: 24+5+35=64.'),('A1','C₂H₅⁺ is a plausible fragment at m/z=29.')])],kind='data-based',figure=plot([('',[(29,65),(64,100),(66,33.3)],'#186c93')],'m/z','Relative intensity',xlim=(20,70),ylim=(0,110),sticks=True))
wr('S3.2','ahl','An aldehyde proton pattern','Constructed proton NMR data for a compound of formula C₂H₄O show a three-proton doublet near 2.2 ppm and a one-proton quartet near 9.7 ppm. Treat coupling between these proton sets as resolved.',[('Identify the compound and assign the signals.',[('A1','Ethanal, CH₃CHO.'),('A1','The methyl gives the 3H signal and the aldehyde proton gives the 1H signal.')]),('Explain why the signals are a doublet and quartet.',[('R1','The methyl protons couple to one adjacent aldehyde proton, giving n+1=2.'),('R1','The aldehyde proton couples to three equivalent methyl protons, giving n+1=4.')])],kind='data-based',figure=plot([('',[(2.17,1.5),(2.23,1.5),(9.61,0.125),(9.67,0.375),(9.73,0.375),(9.79,0.125)],'#186c93')],'Chemical shift / ppm','Signal (arbitrary)',xlim=(11,0),ylim=(0,2),title='Constructed splitting pattern; not an experimental spectrum',sticks=True))
wr('S3.2','sl','Homologous series reasoning','Methanol, ethanol and propan-1-ol belong to the same homologous series.',[('State two defining features of a homologous series illustrated by these compounds.',[('A1','They have the same functional-group type / general formula.'),('A1','Successive members differ by CH₂ and show similar chemical reactions.')]),('Explain why their boiling temperatures tend to increase with chain length while their water solubility tends to decrease.',[('R1','Larger electron clouds increase London dispersion forces and thus boiling temperatures.'),('R1','The non-polar hydrocarbon portion becomes larger relative to the one hydroxy group, making hydration less favourable.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S3.2','ahl','Eliminating candidate structures','A liquid has formula C₄H₈O₂. Its proton NMR spectrum has three signals: a 3H singlet, a 2H quartet and a 3H triplet. The quartet is near 4.1 ppm; a strong IR C=O band is present. Data are constructed.',[('Deduce its structure and assign the three proton sets.',[('A1','Ethyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₂CH₃.'),('A1','CH₃CO gives the singlet; OCH₂ gives the quartet; terminal CH₃ gives the triplet.')]),('Explain why methyl propanoate does not fit the chemical-shift assignment, despite having the same signal integrals and splitting types.',[('R1','Methyl propanoate has OCH₃, so its oxygen-bound proton set is a 3H singlet.'),('R1','Its 2H quartet is adjacent to carbonyl carbon rather than directly to oxygen, so a 2H quartet near 4.1 ppm favours ethyl ethanoate.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('S3.2','ahl','An IR comparison','Constructed IR observations for two C₃H₈O isomers are supplied.'+table(['Sample','Broad 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ band','C=O region'],[('P','Present','No strong band'),('Q','Absent','No strong band')]),[('Give a functional-group assignment for each sample and one possible structure for each.',[('A1','P is an alcohol, for example CH₃CH₂CH₂OH.'),('A1','Q is an ether, CH₃OCH₂CH₃, under the specified C₃H₈O isomer restriction.')]),('Explain why these IR observations alone cannot distinguish propan-1-ol from propan-2-ol.',[('R1','Both contain the same O–H functional-group type and lack C=O.'),('R1','Additional information, such as proton NMR environments/splitting, is required to determine the position of OH.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('S3.1','sl','Hydrogen in two compounds',r'Consider $\mathrm{NaH}$ and $\mathrm{HCl}$.',[('Determine the oxidation state of hydrogen in each and explain the difference.',[('A1','NaH: H is −1; HCl: H is +1.'),('R1','Hydrogen is more electronegative than sodium but less electronegative than chlorine, so electrons are assigned differently in oxidation-state bookkeeping.')]),('State why oxidation state is not always the same as the measured charge on an atom in a covalent molecule.',[('R1','Oxidation states use a formal complete-electron-transfer assignment to the more electronegative atom.'),('R1','Covalent bonding shares electron density, so real partial charges need not be those integer values.')])])
wr('S3.2','ahl','Fragment evidence without overclaiming','A compound gives a molecular-ion peak at m/z=72 and a prominent fragment at m/z=43. Its IR spectrum shows a strong carbonyl band and no broad O–H. Use C=12, H=1 and O=16.',[('Give a plausible formula for a carbonyl-containing fragment at m/z=43 and one ketone structure consistent with molecular mass 72.',[('A1','CH₃CO⁺ (C₂H₃O⁺): 24+3+16=43.'),('A1','Butan-2-one, CH₃COCH₂CH₃, has molecular formula C₄H₈O and mass 72.')]),('Evaluate the claim that a peak at m/z=43 alone uniquely identifies this ketone.',[('R1','The claim is not justified: different compounds can produce the same fragment, and different fragment formulas can have the same nominal mass.'),('R1','Combine molecular mass, IR and NMR or other evidence before making a unique assignment.')])],kind='data-based')
save('S3')
